{% glossary %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="additional-medicare-tax"
     name="Additional Medicare tax" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Flat rate federal tax that applies to Medicare wages over a certain threshold in addition to the regular Medicare tax.
    See {% formLink formClass="f8959" %}Form 8959{% /formLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Additional Medicare tax (IRS topic 560)](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc560)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="adjusted-gross-income"
     name="Adjusted gross income (AGI)" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Amount of {% glossaryLink term="gross-income" %}gross income{% /glossaryLink %} after applying {% glossaryLink term="adjustment" %}adjustments{% /glossaryLink %}.
    After computing this value, you subtract {% glossaryLink term="deduction" %}deductions{% /glossaryLink %} from it to get your {% glossaryLink term="taxable-income" %}taxable income{% /glossaryLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Adjusted gross income (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/filing/adjusted-gross-income) • [adjusted gross income (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/adjusted_gross_income_%28agi%29)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="adjustment" name="Adjustment" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Amount added to or subtracted from {% glossaryLink term="gross-income" %}gross income{% /glossaryLink %} to compute {% glossaryLink term="adjusted-gross-income" %}adjusted gross income{% /glossaryLink %}.
    The law designates certain categories of expenses, contributions, and received payments as adjustments, enumerated in {% formLink formClass="f1040s1" %}Schedule 1 (Form 1040){% /formLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="alternative-minimum-tax"
     name="Alternative minimum tax (AMT)" %}
  - {% definition %}
    {% glossaryLink term="income-tax" %}Income tax{% /glossaryLink %} that applies to higher-income taxpayers in addition to regular {% glossaryLink term="federal-income-tax" %}federal income tax{% /glossaryLink %}.
    The AMT is designed to ensure that taxpayers who receive a lot of tax benefits still pay some amount of tax.
    It only applies when your income is over a certain threshold.

    Specifically, the AMT is the excess of the **tentative minimum tax** over the regular tax.
    The tentative minimum tax is calculated similarly to the regular tax, but you add back certain deductions and exemptions to your {% glossaryLink term="taxable-income" %}taxable income{% /glossaryLink %} first.
    The full calculation is done on {% formLink formClass="f6251" %}Form 6251{% /formLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [alternative minimum tax (AMT) (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/alternative_minimum_tax_%28amt%29) • [Alternative Minimum Tax (IRS topic 556)](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc556)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="backup-withholding" name="Backup withholding" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Type of {% glossaryLink term="withholding" %}withholding{% /glossaryLink %} required when you didn't give a payer the correct taxpayer identification number or when you underreported interest or dividends to the IRS.
    It can apply to most types of payments reported on Form 1099 ({% formLink formClass="f1099B" %}Form 1099-B{% /formLink %}, {% formLink formClass="f1099DIV" %}Form 1099-DIV{% /formLink %}, etc.).
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Backup withholding (IRS topic 307)](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc307)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="capital-gain" name="Capital gain" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Profit from selling capital assets, such as a home, a vehicle, stocks, or bonds.
    Capital gain is considered {% glossaryLink term="income" %}income{% /glossaryLink %} and subject to {% glossaryLink term="income-tax" %}income tax{% /glossaryLink %}.

    You earn capital gain when you sell the asset for more than it cost you to buy it.
    If you sell it for less than it cost, then it's a **capital loss,** which can decrease your income and income tax up to a limit.

    You have to sell ("realize") the asset in order to incur capital gain or loss.
    Changes in the asset's value while you still own it don't count as income.

    The tax rate for capital gains differs depending on how long you held the asset.
    Typically, **short-term capital gains** are held for one year or less and are taxed as regular income.
    **Long-term capital gains** are held for more than one year and are taxed at a lower rate.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Capital gains and losses (IRS topic 409)](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409) • [capital gains (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/capital_gains)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="collectibles" name="Collectibles" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Category of capital asset that includes works of art, stamps, coins, cards, precious metals and gemstones, antiques, and other rare items.
    Long-term {% glossaryLink term="capital-gain" %}capital gains{% /glossaryLink %} earned from selling collectibles are taxed at a special rate, up to 28%.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [How Collectibles Are Taxed (Investopedia)](https://www.investopedia.com/articles/personal-finance/061715/how-are-collectibles-taxed.asp)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="credit" name="Credit" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Amount subtracted from {% glossaryLink term="tax-liability" %}tax liability{% /glossaryLink %} to compute {% glossaryLink term="tax-due" %}tax due{% /glossaryLink %}.
    Examples of tax credits include the [child tax credit](https://www.irs.gov/credits-deductions/individuals/child-tax-credit) and [clean vehicle credits](https://www.irs.gov/clean-vehicle-tax-credits).
    **Deductions** reduce your {% glossaryLink term="taxable-income" %}taxable income{% /glossaryLink %}, while **credits** reduce how much you ultimately owe the IRS.

    **Refundable credits** can increase your {% glossaryLink term="tax-refund" %}tax refund{% /glossaryLink %} if your credits are more than your tax liability.
    They can essentially give you negative total tax liability (a refund).
    On the other hand, **nonrefundable credits** can only reduce your tax liability to zero.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Credits and deductions (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/credits-and-deductions) • [tax credit (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/tax_credit)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="deduction" name="Deduction" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Amount subtracted from {% glossaryLink term="adjusted-gross-income" %}adjusted gross income{% /glossaryLink %} to compute {% glossaryLink term="taxable-income" %}taxable income{% /glossaryLink %}.
    You can typically deduct things like capital losses, business expenses, healthcare costs, other taxes you paid, and donations to charity, up to a limit.
    **Deductions** reduce your taxable income, while **credits** reduce how much you ultimately owe the IRS.

    For {% glossaryLink term="federal-income-tax" %}federal income tax{% /glossaryLink %}, you choose between the **standard deduction** (a fixed amount) and **itemized deductions** (precise amounts computed in {% formLink formClass="f1040sA" %}Schedule A (Form 1040){% /formLink %}) depending on which is larger.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Credits and deductions (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/credits-and-deductions) • [deduction (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/deduction)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="dividend" name="Dividend" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Payment from a corporation to its shareholders.
    Corporations often invest some of their profits back into the company and distribute the rest as dividends.

    {% glossaryLink term="ordinary-dividends" %}Ordinary{% /glossaryLink %} and {% glossaryLink term="qualified-dividends" %}qualified dividends{% /glossaryLink %} are taxed at different rates.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Dividends and other corporate distributions (IRS topic 404)](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc404) • [dividend (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/dividend)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="federal-income-tax" name="Federal income tax" %}
  - {% definition %}
    {% glossaryLink term="income-tax" %}Income tax{% /glossaryLink %} levied by the federal government.
    It is a **progressive tax,** meaning the tax rate increases as your income increases.
    In particular, your income is separated into brackets, and the money in each bracket is taxed at a specific percentage.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Your Federal Income Tax (For Individuals) (IRS pub. 17)](https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-publication-17) • [Federal income tax rates and brackets (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/filing/federal-income-tax-rates-and-brackets)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="golden-parachute" name="Golden parachute" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Benefits that a company agrees to give its executives if they lose their jobs after a merger with or takeover by another company.
    These can include various forms of payment (such as cash, stock options, or immediate vesting) and other benefits (such as staying enrolled in pensions or insurance plans).

    **Excess** golden parachute payments are the amount that golden parachute payments exceed one's average annual compensation in a recent time range.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Understanding Golden Parachutes: Definition, Benefits & Controversy (Investopedia)](https://www.investopedia.com/terms/g/goldenparachute.asp) • [golden parachute (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/golden_parachute) • [§ 1.280G-1 Golden parachute payments (eCFR.gov)](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-26/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subject-group-ECFR210006225231fb0/section-1.280G-1)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="gross-income" name="Gross income" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Also called **total income.**

    For **individuals,** total amount of {% glossaryLink term="income" %}income{% /glossaryLink %} during the tax year.
    This generally includes all income from almost all sources, such as {% glossaryLink term="wages" %}wages{% /glossaryLink %}, {% glossaryLink term="capital-gain" %}capital gains{% /glossaryLink %}, and {% glossaryLink term="dividend" %}dividends{% /glossaryLink %}.
    However, certain sources are excluded, such as gifts and child support.

    After you compute your gross income, you apply {% glossaryLink term="adjustment" %}adjustments{% /glossaryLink %} to get your {% glossaryLink term="adjusted-gross-income" %}adjusted gross income{% /glossaryLink %}.

    For **businesses,** revenue minus cost of goods sold.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [gross income (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/gross_income)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="income" name="Income" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Generally speaking, any money that you receive.
    For the purpose of taxation, this can also include the cash value of non-monetary things, such as physical gifts.
    {% /definition %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="income-tax" name="Income tax" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Tax you pay when you earn {% glossaryLink term="income" %}income{% /glossaryLink %}.
    The annual tax return process in the U.S. centers on figuring out how much income tax you still owe (or how much you overpaid) for the past tax year.
    Other types of taxes include sales tax, which you pay when you buy something, and property tax, which you pay when you own property.
    {% /definition %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="modified-adjusted-gross-income"
     name="Modified adjusted gross income (MAGI)" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Value computed by adding certain amounts to {% glossaryLink term="adjusted-gross-income" %}adjusted gross income (AGI){% /glossaryLink %}.
    You might calculate a MAGI to determine whether you qualify for a tax benefit, how much you can contribute to a retirement or investment account, or whether you owe a particular tax.

    The specific amounts to add to AGI differ depending on what the MAGI is for.
    For example, to compute the MAGI for {% glossaryLink term="net-investment-income-tax" %}net investment income tax{% /glossaryLink %}, you add back any foreign earned income which you originally excluded, among other things.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Modified adjusted gross income (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/credits-deductions/modified-adjusted-gross-income)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="net-investment-income-tax"
     name="Net investment income tax (NIIT)" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Flat rate federal tax on your net investment income or the excess of your {% glossaryLink term="modified-adjusted-gross-income" %}modified adjusted gross income{% /glossaryLink %} over a threshold, whichever is smaller.
    See {% formLink formClass="f8960" %}Form 8960{% /formLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Net investment income tax (IRS topic 559)](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="ordinary-dividends" name="Ordinary dividends" %}
  - {% definition %}
    {% glossaryLink term="dividend" %}Dividends{% /glossaryLink %} that are not {% glossaryLink term="qualified-dividends" %}qualified dividends{% /glossaryLink %}.
    Ordinary dividends are {% glossaryLink term="income-tax" %}taxed as regular income{% /glossaryLink %}, whereas qualified dividends are taxed as {% glossaryLink term="capital-gain" %}capital gains{% /glossaryLink %}.
    In other words, dividends are ordinary by default unless they "qualify" for the capital gains tax by meeting certain conditions.

    If you receive dividends, the payer reports what amount is ordinary vs. qualified on {% formLink formClass="f1099DIV" %}Form 1099-DIV{% /formLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [What Are Qualified Dividends, and How Are They Taxed? (Investopedia)](https://www.investopedia.com/terms/q/qualifieddividend.asp)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="qualified-business-income"
     name="Qualified business income (QBI)" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Broadly, income from self-employment or small business ownership, excluding certain items and subject to certain conditions and limits.
    Eligible individuals can {% glossaryLink term="deduction" %}deduct{% /glossaryLink %} their QBI to reduce their taxes.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Qualified business income deduction (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/qualified-business-income-deduction)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="qualified-dividends" name="Qualified dividends" %}
  - {% definition %}
    {% glossaryLink term="dividend" %}Dividends{% /glossaryLink %} that "qualify" as {% glossaryLink term="capital-gain" %}capital gains{% /glossaryLink %} instead of ordinary income ({% glossaryLink term="ordinary-dividends" %}ordinary dividends{% /glossaryLink %}).
    If you receive dividends, the payer reports what amount is ordinary vs. qualified on {% formLink formClass="f1099DIV" %}Form 1099-DIV{% /formLink %}.

    Specifically, a dividend is qualified if you held the stock for more than 60 days in the 121 day period starting 60 days before its **ex-dividend date,** which is one market day before its **record date,** which is the date that you must be marked as a shareholder in the company's records in order to receive the dividend.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [What Are Qualified Dividends, and How Are They Taxed? (Investopedia)](https://www.investopedia.com/terms/q/qualifieddividend.asp)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="qualified-opportunity-fund"
     name="Qualified Opportunity Fund (QOF)" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Fund that invests in **Qualified Opportunity Zones,** which are "economically distressed communities" designated by the U.S. government.
    QOFs are meant to incentivize investment into these regions, so you can defer taxes on contributions you make to a QOF.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Invest in a Qualified Opportunity Fund (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/credits-deductions/businesses/invest-in-a-qualified-opportunity-fund)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="qualified-small-business-stock"
     name="Qualified small business stock" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Shares in a qualified small business that have tax benefits for the {% glossaryLink term="capital-gain" %}capital gains{% /glossaryLink %} tax under certain conditions.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Qualified Small Business Stock (QSBS): Definition and Tax Benefits (Investopedia)](https://www.investopedia.com/terms/q/qsbs-qualified-small-business-stock.asp)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="recapture" name="Recapture" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Decrease in a tax benefit, such as a {% glossaryLink term="deduction" %}deduction{% /glossaryLink %} or {% glossaryLink term="credit" %}credit{% /glossaryLink %}, because you stopped meeting the required conditions.
    For example, you can get a tax credit from selling certain assets, but if you sell it too soon, then the credit is reduced.
    To be precise, the tax benefit lowers your tax, while the recapture raises your tax again to offset part of the benefit.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [recapture (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/recapture) • [Depreciation recapture tax (Thomson Reuters)](https://tax.thomsonreuters.com/en/glossary/depreciation-recapture-tax)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="section-1202" name="Section 1202" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Section of the U.S. tax code that allows individuals to exclude from {% glossaryLink term="gross-income" %}gross income{% /glossaryLink %} certain gains from the sale or exchange of {% glossaryLink term="qualified-small-business-stock" %}qualified small business stock{% /glossaryLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [26 U.S. Code § 1202 - Partial exclusion for gain from certain small business stock (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1202)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="section-1250" name="Section 1250" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Section of the U.S. tax code that describes the tax treatment of certain real estate gains.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [26 U.S. Code § 1250 - Gain from dispositions of certain depreciable realty (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1250)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="section-1411" name="Section 1411" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Section of the U.S. tax code that imposes the {% glossaryLink term="net-investment-income-tax" %}net investment income tax{% /glossaryLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [26 U.S. Code § 1411 - Imposition of tax (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1411)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="section-199A" name="Section 199A" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Section of the U.S. tax code that allows individuals to {% glossaryLink term="deduction" %}deduct{% /glossaryLink %} their {% glossaryLink term="qualified-business-income" %}qualified business income{% /glossaryLink %}, up to a limit.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [26 U.S. Code § 199A - Qualified business income (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/199A)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="section-897" name="Section 897" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Section of the U.S. tax code that requires foreign individuals or corporations who own real estate in the U.S. to pay property tax on it.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [26 U.S. Code § 897 - Disposition of investment in United States real property (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/897)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="security" name="Security" %}
  - {% definition %}
    In finance, a financial instrument with monetary value, such as stocks and bonds.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [security (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/security) • [What Are Financial Securities? (Investopedia)](https://www.investopedia.com/terms/s/security.asp)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry
     term="specified-private-activity-bond"
     name="Specified private activity bond" %}
  - {% definition %}
    A **private activity bond** is a bond issued to fund private business activity.

    A **specified** private activity bond is one whose interest isn't included in {% glossaryLink term="gross-income" %}gross income{% /glossaryLink %} under certain conditions.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [26 U.S. Code § 57(a)(5)(C) - Specified private activity bonds (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/57#a_5_C) • [26 U.S. Code § 141 - Private activity bond; qualified bond (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/141)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="tax-due" name="Tax due" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Amount you must pay to the IRS for the tax year.
    If you already paid more than this amount, for example through {% glossaryLink term="withholding" %}withholding{% /glossaryLink %} on your wages, then you'll receive a {% glossaryLink term="tax-refund" %}tax refund{% /glossaryLink %} instead.
    {% /definition %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="tax-liability" name="Tax liability" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Also called **tax obligation.**

    Sum of taxes you owe for the tax year.
    This can be reduced by {% glossaryLink term="credit" %}tax credits{% /glossaryLink %} to get your actual {% glossaryLink term="tax-due" %}tax due{% /glossaryLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="tax-refund" name="Tax refund" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Amount the IRS pays back to you for the tax year.
    This is how much you paid them in excess of your {% glossaryLink term="tax-due" %}tax due{% /glossaryLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="taxable-income" name="Taxable income" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Amount of {% glossaryLink term="income" %}income{% /glossaryLink %} used as the basis for computing {% glossaryLink term="income-tax" %}income tax{% /glossaryLink %}.
    For {% glossaryLink term="federal-income-tax" %}federal income tax{% /glossaryLink %}, this is the amount left over after subtracting {% glossaryLink term="deduction" %}deductions{% /glossaryLink %} from your {% glossaryLink term="adjusted-gross-income" %}adjusted gross income{% /glossaryLink %}.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Taxable income (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/filing/taxable-income) • [What is taxable and nontaxable income? (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-is-taxable-and-nontaxable-income) • [taxable income (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/taxable_income)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="wages" name="Wages" %}
  - {% definition %}
    {% glossaryLink term="income" %}Income{% /glossaryLink %} that an employee receives from their employer in exchange for their labor.
    This includes essentially all forms of compensation: base pay, bonuses, commissions, tips, company equity, and other benefits.

    There are exceptions for certain types of labor, such as agricultural labor, or employers, such as the federal government.
    In these cases the employee's income might be computed differently.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Wages and salaries (IRS topic 401)](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc401) • [wages (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/wages)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="wash-sale" name="Wash sale" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Act of selling a {% glossaryLink term="security" %}security{% /glossaryLink %} at a loss and buying a "substantially identical" security within 30 days before or after the sale.
    You can't claim {% glossaryLink term="deduction" %}tax deductions{% /glossaryLink %} for losses from wash sales.
    This rule is intended to prevent people from selling and immediately buying back securities just to reduce their taxes.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Wash Sales (Investor.gov)](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/wash-sales) • [wash sale (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/wash_sale)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
- {% glossaryEntry term="withholding" name="Withholding" %}
  - {% definition %}
    Practice where someone paying {% glossaryLink term="income" %}income{% /glossaryLink %} to you sends a portion of the payment to the government to pay {% glossaryLink term="income-tax" %}income tax{% /glossaryLink %} on your behalf.
    For example, in the U.S., employers are usually required to withhold {% glossaryLink term="federal-income-tax" %}federal income tax{% /glossaryLink %} on your {% glossaryLink term="wages" %}wages{% /glossaryLink %} and report the withheld amount on {% formLink formClass="fW2" %}Form W-2{% /formLink %}.

    Withholding doesn't change your {% glossaryLink term="tax-liability" %}tax liability{% /glossaryLink %}; any withheld amount still counts as part of your {% glossaryLink term="gross-income" %}gross income{% /glossaryLink %}.
    It just lets the government collect the money sooner.

    Different income taxes have different withholding rules governing who should withhold, when, and how much.
    Not all income sources withhold taxes, and if you have multiple income sources then the combined withheld amount might be less than the actual tax you owe (because the income tax rate increases as your income increases).
    So, it's important to plan ahead in order to avoid owing a large amount at the end of the tax year.
    {% /definition %}
  - {% learnMore %}
    [Tax withholding (IRS.gov)](https://www.irs.gov/individuals/employees/tax-withholding) • [tax withholding (Cornell LII)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/tax_withholding)
    {% /learnMore %}
  {% /glossaryEntry %}
{% /glossary %}
